+Three findings drove withdrawal rather than rescue:
+
+* A `disabled` reading was never proof of disabled quota. The btrfs reading
+  rests on a **partial** `/sys/fs/btrfs` listing: on this host that listing holds
+  the uuid directory plus the non-uuid `features/` sibling, which lists no
+  device, so "exactly one uuid lists the device" and "that uuid has no `qgroups`
+  directory" are only as complete as the listing that was readable. A
+  permission-limited or partial listing cannot be distinguished from an absent
+  `qgroups` directory, so a denied listing must never be read as absent — that is
+  the false-`disabled` route, recorded by the sentinel before the seal.
+* tmpfs accounting is not runtime enforcement. `quota`/`usrquota`/`grpquota`
+  evidence that quota accounting and enforcement were requested for that mount;
+  they do not evidence that any limit was armed or that EDQUOT could occur, and
+  the tmpfs case additionally carries a selected-source provenance erratum.
+* The conservative refusal rules are right but unprovable against the frozen
+  expectations: the sealed parent-ID rule demands `ambiguous_overmount` where the
+  frozen case expects an enabled row, so the strict target cannot be met without
+  editing a sealed expectation, which is forbidden.
+
+Withdrawal is not a clearance and not a repair. No quota configuration, cause,
+limit, usage or EDQUOT attribution is claimed or diagnosed here, and no census
+cutover or threshold change follows. Configuration and causes belong to
+tsukuroi/operator: no fstab, mount unit, Snapper, superblock, quotactl, btrfs,
+xfs_quota, repquota, mount or remediation is read or implied. Four retention
+hits, permission 11/12 red, two unknown dispositions and blocked baseline
+acceptance remain unchanged authority; this withdrawal grants no clearance.
